WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:06.020
In this session, we will look at

2
00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:09.980
inferential statistics:
how to construct a test.

3
00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:14.360
First, when we want to construct a test,

4
00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:18.660
we take a sample intended to represent

5
00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:22.520
the underlying population,
which we cannot study in full.

6
00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:28.440
The sample must be representative
or follow a known

7
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:31.880
distribution so that we can use
it to carry out a test.

8
00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:34.500
Familiar distributions include
the normal distribution,

9
00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.440
and the chi, F and t
distributions, for example.

10
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:43.500
The sample must also be independent,

11
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.400
meaning that observations
must be collected

12
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.800
independently of one another and follow

13
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:56.880
the same theoretical distribution,
normal or otherwise.

14
00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.720
Once we have this sample,
which we want to be

15
00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:07.280
as large as possible,
we calculate its characteristic values:

16
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:10.560
sample statistics such as the mean,

17
00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:14.800
the standard deviation, or a t
or chi test statistic, for example.

18
00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:19.660
Based on what we observe
in the sample, we can

19
00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:25.700
reject a hypothesis
that we formulated at the outset,

20
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:30.540
and use that rejection to make

21
00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:36.040
what we believe is a sound decision
about the original population.

22
00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:40.280
Since we do not know
the underlying distribution,

23
00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:43.380
we rely on the values we can obtain

24
00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.400
from the sample to make our decisions.
